## 7. SCREEN-BY-SCREEN UX RULES

**Every screen must have:**
1. One primary action (one PrimaryButton or a clear next step)
2. A heading that states what the screen is for
3. Visible progress if inside onboarding or the questionnaire
4. An obvious back path — never trap the user
5. A designed loading/empty state, never a bare spinner

### Splash
- Brand wordmark centred, `deepNavy` on `iceBlue`; tagline in `slate` below.
- A clean fade-in only. No production animation sequence.

### Onboarding (3 screens max)
- One idea per screen, illustrated with a simple Feather composition.
- Horizontal swipe, dot indicators. "Skip" TextButton top-right from screen 2.
- Final CTA: "Get started".

### Auth
- Single column, 20px padding. Email first, password below, generous spacing.
- Show-password eye icon in the field.
- Errors inline below the field, `riskHighText`, 13px.
- "Forgot password" TextButton below password on login.
- **No social/Google login** — health data plus Google is a trust concern.

### Triage (the most important screen in the app)
- Calm background. Never alarming.
- Measured H1 ("Before we begin") that sets an intentional tone.
- Subheading explains why one important question is being asked.
- Options as full-width radio cards, with generous space before the CTA so
  nobody taps by accident.
- CTA enabled only when every question is answered.

### Questionnaire sections
- Stepper pinned at top, always visible.
- One section per screen. Never multiple topics in one scroll.
- Labels above inputs, never placeholder-only.
- "Why do we ask this?" info affordance on anything that may feel intrusive,
  opening a bottom sheet with one plain paragraph.
- "Save & continue" pinned at the bottom, always reachable.
- Keyboard-aware: the CTA stays visible.
- After save: a completion moment (~1.5s), then auto-advance.
- Back returns to the questionnaire hub, not the previous section — sections
  autosave, so there is no "back = undo" expectation to manage.

### Results
- Status chips lead; the value lives in the detail.
- Group by status — "Worth watching" first, then "Within range". Never
  alphabetical and never by test tier; users do not think in tiers.
- No red unless a result is genuinely critical. Most users should see amber
  at worst, and the design must not pre-alarm them.

### Personalised plan
- "Pending review" banner uses the **success** tone, not a warning: a
  clinician looking at your results is a good thing.
- Group by Daily habits / Food & drink / Supplements / Follow-up tests /
  See a specialist. Never by clinical category.
- Each card: action title first, reason second, clinical basis collapsed.
- Empty state is future-positive and explains what happens next.

### Store
- "Based on your results" section at top, max 5 items, filtered to this
  user's triggers. Not "Recommended products".
- Product grid, two columns on phone, for the full catalogue.
- Safety states (blocked/flagged) styled as **information, not errors**.

### Pathway B exit
- **Never a modal. Full screen.** This deserves full attention.
- Calm, warm, directive. Not a red screen of doom.

